<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gretl-functions>
<gretl-function-package name="decomposition" minver="2023c">
<author email="atecon@posteo.de">Artur Tarassow</author>
<version>0.1</version>
<date>2026-10-11</date>
<description>Seasonal decomposition of time-series</description>
<tags>C22</tags>
<help filename="decomposition_help.md">
# decomposition Package

This package decomposes a seasonal time series into a trend-cycle, a seasonal and a remainder component using classical decomposition (additive or multiplicative), which is based on centered moving averages. For more information see:

https://otexts.com/fpp3/classical-decomposition.html

The series must have a seasonal (periodic) frequency, e.g. quarterly or monthly data. The seasonal period is taken from the dataset frequency.

Please ask questions and report bugs on the Gretl mailing list if possible. Alternatively, create an issue ticket on the github repo (see below).
Source code and test script(s) can be found here: https://github.com/atecon/decomposition


# Public Functions

## clc_decompose

```
clc_decompose (series y, bundle opts[null])
```

Classical decomposition of a time series into trend-cycle, seasonal and remainder component.

The trend-cycle is estimated by a centered moving average (an m-MA if the seasonal period m is odd, a 2xm-MA if m is even). The seasonal component is the average of the detrended series for each season, centered such that the seasonal indices sum to zero (additive) or average to one (multiplicative). The remainder is what is left over. Missing values at the sample edges are ignored with a warning. The series must contain at least two full seasonal cycles.

For the additive model: `y = trend + season + remainder`. For the multiplicative model: `y = trend * season * remainder`.

**Arguments:**

- `y`: series, Series to decompose.
- `opts`: bundle, Optional parameters. **You can pass the following parameters:**

    * `multiplicative`: bool, If `TRUE` use the multiplicative model, otherwise the additive model (default: `FALSE`).
    * `extrapolate_trend`: int, If greater than zero, the trend-cycle is linearly extrapolated into the missing values which the moving average leaves at both ends of the series. A line is fitted by OLS to the nearest `extrapolate_trend + 1` valid trend points at each end. As the seasonal and remainder components are computed from the trend, they are then also available at the edges. A larger value gives a smoother, less noise-sensitive extrapolation. The default `0` leaves the missing values in place (default: `0`).

**Return:** Bundle holding various items.

- `trend`: series, Trend-cycle component.
- `season`: series, Seasonal component.
- `remainder`: series, Remainder component.
- `y`: series, The decomposed series.
- `frequency`: scalar, Seasonal period of the data.
- `nobs`: scalar, Number of observations used.
- `multiplicative`: bool, Model type used.
- `extrapolate_trend`: int, Number of extra points used for extrapolating the trend.

**Note:** Without trend extrapolation the first and last `m/2` observations of `trend` and `remainder` are missing (`m` is the seasonal period).



## plot_decompose

```
plot_decompose (series target, bundle Model, bundle opts[null])
```

Plot the components of a decomposition as a grid plot with three rows: (1) the actual series together with the trend-cycle, (2) the seasonal component and (3) the remainder component. Each subplot has its own title. The seasonal and the remainder subplots include a horizontal reference line at zero (additive model) or at one (multiplicative model).

**Arguments:**

- `target`: series, The series that was decomposed.
- `Model`: bundle, Model object returned by the `*_decompose()` function.
- `opts`: bundle, Optional parameters for controlling the plot. **You can pass the following parameters:**

    * `output`: string, Path and name of the file to save the plot. The file type is determined by the file extension (e.g. `.png` or `.pdf`). If not provided, the plot is shown on the screen (default: show on screen).
    * `title`: string, Overall title of the plot (default: no title).
    * `width`: scalar, Width of the plot in pixels (default: 800).
    * `height`: scalar, Height of the plot in pixels (default: 900).
    * `linewidth`: scalar, Line width (default: 1.0).
    * `monochrome`: bool, If `TRUE`, draw black lines only. The trend-cycle and the reference lines are then distinguished by their dash pattern (default: `FALSE`).
    * `grid`: bool, If `TRUE`, show grid lines (default: `TRUE`).

**Return:** Scalar, `0` if no error occurred, otherwise `1`.



# Interpretation of the components

Let `y` be the series, e.g. units sold per month, and let `trend`, `season` and `remainder` be the components returned by `clc_decompose()`.

## Additive model

```
y = trend + season + remainder
```

- `trend`: the underlying level, in the units of `y` (e.g. units sold per month).
- `season`: the seasonal effect in units of `y`, centered such that the indices sum to zero over one cycle. A value of `+64` for July means that July sales are on average 64 units above the underlying level. The effect is the same number of units in every year, irrespective of the level of the series.
- `remainder`: the deviation from `trend + season` in units of `y`.
- Seasonally adjusted series: `y - season`.

The additive model is appropriate if the size of the seasonal swings stays roughly constant as the level of the series changes.

## Multiplicative model

```
y = trend * season * remainder
```

- `trend`: the underlying level, in the units of `y`. It is of a similar magnitude as in the additive model.
- `season`: a dimensionless seasonal index, centered such that the indices average to one over one cycle. A value of `1.23` for July means that July sales are on average 23% above the underlying level, whatever that level is. A value below one means that the season is weaker than average.
- `remainder`: a dimensionless factor, fluctuating around one. A value of `0.95` means that the observation is 5% below the value expected from trend and season, a value of `1.03` means 3% above.
- Seasonally adjusted series: `y / season`. This series is in the units of `y`.

The multiplicative model is appropriate if the seasonal swings grow (or shrink) in proportion to the level of the series, i.e. if the seasonal effect is a constant percentage rather than a constant number of units. In this case, the remainder of an additive model typically becomes larger when the level of the series rises, whereas the multiplicative remainder stays at a similar relative size.

## Converting the multiplicative components to units

The dimensionless components can be expressed in the units of `y`:

```
seasonal effect in units = trend * (season - 1)
remainder in units       = y - trend * season
```

In contrast to the additive model, the seasonal effect in units varies over time with the level of the trend. For instance, a seasonal index of `1.23` corresponds to `+29` units at a trend of `127` units and to `+110` units at a trend of `475` units.

## Choosing between the two models

Plot the series. If the seasonal swings widen as the level rises, use the multiplicative model (`multiplicative = TRUE`), otherwise use the additive model. Both are linked via logarithms: the multiplicative model for `y` is the additive model for `log(y)`, as `log(y) = log(trend) + log(season) + log(remainder)`. The multiplicative model requires strictly positive values of `y`.


# Changelog

* **v0.1 (October 2026)**
    * initial release: classical decomposition via `clc_decompose()` (including optional trend extrapolation) and the plot function `plot_decomposition()`
</help>
<gretl-function name="clc_decompose" type="bundle">
 <params count="2">
  <param name="y" type="series" const="true"/>
  <param name="opts" type="bundle" optional="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Classical decomposition of a time-series.
https://otexts.com/fpp2/classical-decomposition.html

opts.multiplicative: additive (FALSE, default) or multiplicative
decomposition.

opts.extrapolate_trend: if &gt; 0, linearly extrapolate the
trend-cycle into the NAs left at both ends by the moving average
in trend_component(), using OLS on the nearest
(extrapolate_trend+1) valid trend points at each end. This also
removes the corresponding NAs from detrended/season/remainder, since they are all computed downstream of trend. Mirrors the
extrapolate_trend parameter of Python's
statsmodels.tsa.seasonal.seasonal_decompose(); R's
stats::decompose() (which fpp3's classical_decomposition() wraps)
has no equivalent and always leaves the edge NAs in place, which
is this function's default (extrapolate_trend=0) behavior too. */

bundle self = set_bundle_and_get_defaults(opts)

scalar Torig = $nobs
smpl y --contiguous
scalar T = $nobs
if T &lt; Torig
  printf &quot;Warning: %d missing observations were ignored.\n&quot;, (Torig - T)
endif
scalar self.frequency = $pd
scalar self.nobs = T

errorif(self.frequency == 1, &quot;Time-series must have seasonal frequency.&quot;)
errorif(T &lt; 2 * self.frequency, sprintf(&quot;Time-series must have at least %d valid observations.&quot;, 2 * self.frequency))

series trend = trend_component(y)
trend = extrapolate_trend_component(trend, self.extrapolate_trend)
series detrended = detrended_component(y, trend, self.multiplicative)
series season = season_component(detrended, self.multiplicative)
series remainder = remainder_component(y, trend, season, self.multiplicative)
setinfo trend --description=&quot;Trend-cycle component (classical decomposition)&quot; --graph-name=&quot;Trend-cycle&quot;
setinfo season --description=&quot;Seasonal component (classical decomposition)&quot; --graph-name=&quot;Seasonal&quot;
setinfo remainder --description=&quot;Remainder component (classical decomposition)&quot; --graph-name=&quot;Remainder&quot;

series self.trend = trend
series self.season = season
series self.remainder = remainder
series self.y = y

return self
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="plot_decompose" type="scalar">
 <params count="3">
  <param name="target" type="series" const="true"/>
  <param name="Model" type="bundle" const="true"/>
  <param name="opts" type="bundle" optional="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Plot the components of a classical decomposition as a grid plot
with three rows: (1) actual and trend series, (2) seasonal
component, (3) remainder component. The seasonal and the remainder
subplots include a horizontal reference line at zero (additive
model) or one (multiplicative model).

Model: bundle returned by clc_decompose().

opts.output: path+filename of the output file; if not set or empty, the plot is shown on screen.
opts.width, opts.height: size of the grid plot in pixels.
opts.title: overall title of the plot (default: none).
opts.linewidth: line width (default: 1.0).
opts.monochrome: black lines only (default: FALSE).
opts.grid: show grid lines (default: TRUE).

Returns 0 if no error occurs, otherwise 1. */

# check the input bundle
strings required = defarray(&quot;trend&quot;, &quot;season&quot;, &quot;remainder&quot;, &quot;multiplicative&quot;)
loop i = 1..nelem(required)
  if !inbundle(Model, required[i])
    printf &quot;Error: 'Model' is not a bundle returned by clc_decompose().\n&quot;
    return 1
  endif
endloop
if !exists(opts)
  bundle opts = defbundle()
endif
bundle self = plot_default_values()
self = opts + self          # override defaults

series trend = Model.trend
series season = Model.season
series remainder = Model.remainder
series ref = Model.multiplicative == TRUE ? 1 : 0   # reference line

list Ltrend = target trend
list Lseason = season ref
list Lremainder = remainder ref

# line style: first series, second series (trend or reference line)
scalar lw = self.linewidth
string st1 = sprintf(&quot;set linetype 1 lw %g&quot;, lw)
string st2 = sprintf(&quot;set linetype 2 lw %g&quot;, lw)
string ref_st = &quot;set linetype 2 dt 2 lw 1&quot;
if self.monochrome
  st1 = sprintf(&quot;set linetype 1 lc rgb 'black' lw %g&quot;, lw)
  st2 = sprintf(&quot;set linetype 2 lc rgb 'black' dt 2 lw %g&quot;, lw)
  ref_st = &quot;set linetype 2 lc rgb 'black' dt 3 lw 1&quot;
endif
string grid_st = self.grid ? &quot;set grid&quot; : &quot;unset grid&quot;
scalar ref0 = Model.multiplicative == TRUE ? 1 : 0
scalar max_abs_remainder = max(abs(remainder - ref0))
scalar max_abs_season = max(abs(season - ref0))
scalar season_lo = ref0 - 1.025 * max_abs_season
scalar season_hi = ref0 + 1.025 * max_abs_season
scalar rem_lo = ref0 - 1.025 * max_abs_remainder
scalar rem_hi = ref0 + 1.025 * max_abs_remainder

strings Plots = array(3)

plot Ltrend
  options time-series with-lines single-yaxis
  literal set title &quot;Data and trend-cycle&quot;
  printf &quot;%s&quot;, grid_st
  printf &quot;%s&quot;, st1
  printf &quot;%s&quot;, st2
end plot --outbuf=Plots[1]

plot Lseason
  options time-series with-lines single-yaxis
  literal set title &quot;Seasonal&quot;
  literal set nokey
  printf &quot;%s&quot;, grid_st
  printf &quot;%s&quot;, st1
  printf &quot;%s&quot;, ref_st
  printf &quot;set yrange [%g:%g]&quot;, season_lo, season_hi
end plot --outbuf=Plots[2]

plot Lremainder
  options time-series with-lines single-yaxis
  literal set title &quot;Remainder&quot;
  literal set nokey
  printf &quot;%s&quot;, grid_st
  printf &quot;%s&quot;, st1
  printf &quot;%s&quot;, ref_st
  printf &quot;set yrange [%g:%g]&quot;, rem_lo, rem_hi
end plot --outbuf=Plots[3]

string dest = (strlen(self.output) &gt; 0) ? self.output : &quot;display&quot;
string ttl = self.title

catch gridplot Plots --rows=3 --width=self.width --height=self.height --title=&quot;@ttl&quot; --output=&quot;@dest&quot;

return $error ? 1 : 0
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="default_values" type="bundle" private="1">
<code>/* Set default values for clc_decompose()'s options bundle. */

bundle self = defbundle()
scalar self.multiplicative = FALSE
scalar self.extrapolate_trend = 0

return self
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="set_bundle_and_get_defaults" type="bundle" private="1">
 <params count="1">
  <param name="opts" type="bundle" optional="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Compile self bundle by merging eventual information
from 'opts' bundle. */

if !exists(opts)
  bundle opts = defbundle()
endif

bundle self = default_values()
self = opts + self          # override defaults

return self
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="remainder_component" type="series" private="1">
 <params count="4">
  <param name="y" type="series" const="true"/>
  <param name="trend" type="series" const="true"/>
  <param name="season" type="series" const="true"/>
  <param name="multiplicative" type="bool" const="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Compute the remainder component. */

if multiplicative
  return y / (trend * season)
else
  return y - trend - season
endif
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="season_component" type="series" private="1">
 <params count="2">
  <param name="detrended" type="series" const="true"/>
  <param name="multiplicative" type="bool" const="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Compute seasonal component. */

matrix m = seasonal_means(detrended)

# center the seasonal indices: sum to zero (additive) or
# average to one (multiplicative), as required by the
# classical decomposition method
if multiplicative
  m = m ./ meanc(m)
else
  m = m .- meanc(m)
endif

return lincomb(seasonals(), m')
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="seasonal_means" type="matrix" private="1">
 <params count="1">
  <param name="detrended" type="series" const="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Compute seasonal averages based on detrended series. Clever idea from Oleh. */

series season_id = lincomb(seasonals(), seq(1, $pd))
# mean values for each season id (january, feb etc)
matrix m = aggregate(detrended, season_id, mean)[,3]
return m
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="detrended_component" type="series" private="1">
 <params count="3">
  <param name="y" type="series" const="true"/>
  <param name="trend" type="series" const="true"/>
  <param name="multiplicative" type="bool" const="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Compute detrended component */

if multiplicative
  series season = y / trend
else
  series season = y - trend
endif

return season
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="trend_component" type="series" private="1">
 <params count="1">
  <param name="y" type="series" const="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Compute the trend-cycle component via a centered moving average,
per https://otexts.com/fpp3/classical-decomposition.html (Step 1):
an m-MA if the seasonal period m ($pd) is odd, or a 2xm-MA if m is
even. gretl's movavg(x, p, control) with a non-zero control already
implements the full centered procedure for either case -- for even
p this is the two-stage &quot;m-MA then 2-MA of that&quot; construction, not
a plain (2p)-term average, so no manual doubling of p is needed or
correct. Verified against the worked beer-production example in
the fpp3 textbook (Table 3.2): movavg(beer, 4, 1) reproduces the
published 2x4-MA column exactly. */

series trend = movavg(y, $pd, 1)

return trend
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="extrapolate_trend_component" type="series" private="1">
 <params count="2">
  <param name="trend" type="series"/>
  <param name="n" type="int" const="true"/>
 </params>
<code>/* Linearly extrapolate trend into the NAs left at both ends by
trend_component()'s moving average, via OLS on the nearest (n+1)
valid points at each end. Mirrors statsmodels'
seasonal_decompose(..., extrapolate_trend=n) / its internal
_extrapolate_trend() helper. A no-op when n &lt;= 0 (the default), which preserves the previous behavior of leaving the edge NAs in
place -- matching R's stats::decompose(), which has no equivalent
option.

In plain terms:
find the first and last non-missing trend index (front, back)
if there's a gap before front:
  fit a line through the nearest (n+1) valid trend points
  starting at front, and use it to fill trend[1 .. front-1]
  if there's a gap after back:
    fit a line through the nearest (n+1) valid trend points
    ending at back, and use it to fill trend[back+1 .. T]

    Implemented with matrices and mols() rather than the ols command:
    `set skip_missing off` makes {trend} convert the series to a
    matrix that keeps NAs as NaN placeholders (instead of silently
    dropping those rows, which is gretl's default for series-to-matrix
    conversion), so the matrix stays the same length as the series and
    &quot;which positions are missing&quot; can be read off directly with ok().
    The two edge fits then become plain matrix regressions (mols) and
    the fill-in is a single matrix multiplication, rather than a
    manual per-observation loop. This setting is local to the
    function call and does not affect the caller. */

      if n &lt;= 0
        return trend
      endif

      set skip_missing off
      scalar T = $nobs
      matrix idx = seq(1, T)'
      matrix y = {trend}
      matrix valid = ok(y)

      # indices of all valid (non-missing) trend points; the first and
      # last of these are the front/back edges of the valid region
      matrix valid_idx = selifr(idx, valid)
      if rows(valid_idx) == 0
        # trend has no valid values at all -- nothing to extrapolate
        return trend
      endif
      scalar front = valid_idx[1]
      scalar back = valid_idx[rows(valid_idx)]

      if front &gt; 1
        scalar front_last = min(front + n, back)
        matrix X = idx[front:front_last] ~ ones(front_last - front + 1, 1)
        matrix b = mols(y[front:front_last], X)
        matrix Xfill = idx[1:front-1] ~ ones(front - 1, 1)
        y[1:front-1] = Xfill * b
      endif

      if back &lt; T
        scalar back_first = max(front, back - n)
        matrix X2 = idx[back_first:back] ~ ones(back - back_first + 1, 1)
        matrix b2 = mols(y[back_first:back], X2)
        matrix Xfill2 = idx[back+1:T] ~ ones(T - back, 1)
        y[back+1:T] = Xfill2 * b2
      endif

      series result = y
      return result
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="plot_default_values" type="bundle" private="1">
<code>/* Set default values for clc_plot()'s options bundle. */

bundle self = defbundle()
string self.output = &quot;&quot;         # empty: show plot on screen
string self.title = &quot;&quot;          # empty: no overall title
scalar self.width = 800
scalar self.height = 900
scalar self.linewidth = 1.0
scalar self.monochrome = FALSE
scalar self.grid = TRUE

return self
</code>
</gretl-function>
<sample-script filename="decomposition_package_sample.inp">
clear
set verbose off
include decomposition.gfn

# Monthly airline passengers (Box-Jenkins series G)
open bjg.gdt --quiet
series y = g

# Additive classical decomposition
bundle Model = clc_decompose(y)

series trend = Model.trend
series season = Model.season
series remainder = Model.remainder
list L = trend season remainder
L += y
print L --head=10
scatters L --output=display

# Multiplicative decomposition with trend extrapolation: fills in the
# missing values which the moving average leaves at both ends
bundle opts = defbundle(&quot;multiplicative&quot;, TRUE, &quot;extrapolate_trend&quot;, 3)
bundle ModelExt = clc_decompose(y, opts)

series trend_ext = ModelExt.trend
series season_ext = ModelExt.season
series remainder_ext = ModelExt.remainder
list Lext = trend_ext season_ext remainder_ext
Lext += y
print Lext --head=10

# Plot the components
scalar err = plot_decompose(y, ModelExt)
</sample-script>
</gretl-function-package>
</gretl-functions>
